// Wonder Lab american history data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Years are never `found` — see check_history and .work/BRIEF-ancient.md.
// 48 facts across 8 sections.
const AMERICA = [{"id":"founding-1","section":"founding","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Declaration of Independence is a single sheet of parchment, about 24 by 30 inches, written out by hand. It is in a sealed case of argon gas in Washington. The signatures have faded badly, mostly from years of being displayed in sunlight.","more":"John Hancock's is still the clearest, which is why his name became slang for a signature."},{"id":"founding-2","section":"founding","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Jefferson's rough draft survives with the crossings-out still on it. You can see which words were argued over and what got cut before the final version — an unusual thing to be able to do with any famous document.","tryit":"Look up an image of the rough draft online and find one sentence that was changed. Ask yourself why."},{"id":"founding-3","section":"founding","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Constitution is four sheets of parchment, and it was written out by one man — Jacob Shallus, a clerk, over a weekend, for thirty dollars. His name appears nowhere on it.","more":"He made a few small mistakes and squeezed the corrections into the margins, where they are still visible."},{"id":"founding-4","section":"founding","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Franklin's kite letter of 1752 describes drawing sparks from a thunderstorm with a key on a silk ribbon. He wrote it up for the Royal Society himself, and it is the only account we have of it from anyone who was there.","more":"He was careful to say the silk had to be kept dry, indoors, and that the experimenter must stand under cover. Several people who tried it without that care were killed."},{"id":"founding-5","section":"founding","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Liberty Bell has cracked twice. It cracked on the very first test ring after arriving, was melted down and recast twice by local workmen, and cracked again later. The famous wide slot was cut deliberately, to try to stop the crack spreading.","more":"It did not work, and the bell has not been rung since. You can go and look at it in Philadelphia."},{"id":"founding-6","section":"founding","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"George Washington's false teeth were not wooden. The surviving set is made of hippopotamus ivory, human teeth, brass screws and a lead base. They stained dark over time, which is probably where the wood story came from.","more":"They are in a museum at Mount Vernon. They look painful, and by every account they were."},{"id":"explorers-1","section":"explorers","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"At L'Anse aux Meadows, on the northern tip of Newfoundland, there are the outlines of eight turf buildings, a smithy, iron nails and a bronze cloak pin. They are Norse. They were found in 1960 by a Norwegian couple who went looking after reading the old sagas.","more":"The sagas had described a place across the water called Vinland. Most people assumed it was a story. Helge and Anne Stine Ingstad took it as directions."},{"id":"explorers-2","section":"explorers","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Columbus's own logbook is lost. What survives is a summary that a priest made from a copy. So every quotation from that first voyage has passed through at least two other hands before it reaches you.","more":"He died still believing he had reached the edge of Asia. The continent is named after a different Italian, Amerigo Vespucci. Vespucci said plainly that it was not Asia."},{"id":"explorers-3","section":"explorers","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Lewis and Clark kept journals every day for two and a half years. They came back with 178 plants and 122 animals written up. Nobody in the eastern states had recorded any of them. They brought measurements, drawings and pressed specimens.","more":"The journals survive and are printed in full. You can read what the weather was doing on a given morning in 1805."},{"id":"explorers-4","section":"explorers","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Sacagawea traveled the whole route with a baby son on her back. The journals record what happened when a boat tipped. She was the one who reached out and saved the instruments, books and papers floating away. Those were the records of the entire expedition.","more":"Clark named a river after her and later paid for her son's schooling."},{"id":"explorers-5","section":"explorers","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"John Wesley Powell ran the Colorado River through the Grand Canyon in 1869 in four wooden boats, having lost most of his right arm in the war. Nobody had charted it. Three men left partway through and were never seen again.","more":"He kept a journal in a boat that repeatedly capsized. Enough of it survived to be published."},{"id":"explorers-6","section":"explorers","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Working out how far north you are is easy — measure the sun at noon. Working out how far east or west needs an accurate clock, because you compare your local noon with the time somewhere else. That is why sea clocks mattered so much.","tryit":"At exactly noon, measure the shadow of a stick of known height. Do it again in three months. The change is your latitude changing with the seasons, not you moving."},{"id":"colonies-1","section":"colonies","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"For centuries everyone assumed the river had washed the original Jamestown fort away. In 1994 an archaeologist dug where he thought it should be anyway. He found the postholes of the palisade wall in the first few weeks.","more":"They have since dug up armor, tools, coins, and the graves of some of the first settlers, all inside those walls."},{"id":"colonies-2","section":"colonies","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"The Mayflower carried 102 passengers plus crew on a ship roughly 100 ft long. That is about the length of two buses, for 66 days, with livestock. Living space per person was smaller than a modern parking space.","more":"No plans of the ship survive. The dimensions are worked out from the tonnage recorded for her and from other ships of the same class."},{"id":"colonies-3","section":"colonies","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The log cabin is not an English idea. English colonists built timber-frame houses with sawn boards. Log building came with Swedes and Finns who settled on the Delaware, where stacking whole logs was ordinary at home.","more":"It spread because it needs an axe and trees and almost nothing else — no saw pit, no nails, no carpenter."},{"id":"colonies-4","section":"colonies","cat":"daily","kind":"found","text":"Colonial children learned to read from a hornbook. It was a wooden paddle with one printed sheet on it. Over the sheet went a thin, see-through layer of cow horn, to stop small hands wearing it out. One page, and it had to last.","more":"Surviving hornbooks are in museums. Most carry the alphabet, the vowels, and the Lord's Prayer."},{"id":"colonies-5","section":"colonies","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Benjamin Franklin was apprenticed to his brother's print shop at twelve and was not allowed to write for the paper. So he wrote letters under a false name, a middle-aged widow, and slid them under the door at night. His brother printed them.","more":"They were popular. When Franklin admitted it, his brother was furious."},{"id":"colonies-6","section":"colonies","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Benjamin Banneker taught himself astronomy from borrowed books. He worked out his own tables of sunrise, tides and eclipses. He published them as almanacs. He sent one to Thomas Jefferson with a letter, and Jefferson wrote back.","more":"He also built a striking clock, entirely out of wood, having taken apart a borrowed pocket watch to see how it worked. It kept time for decades."},{"id":"inventors-1","section":"inventors","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"Edison's laboratory left about five million pages of notebooks. They are being scanned and read, and most of what is in them is failure — sketches, dead ends, and lists of things tried and crossed off.","more":"That is the actual record of how the light bulb happened. Hundreds of filament materials were tested, one after another. Each result was written down."},{"id":"inventors-2","section":"inventors","cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Samuel Morse's first long telegraph message, sent from Washington to Baltimore in 1844, was four words: What hath God wrought. The paper tape with those dots and dashes on it still exists.","more":"The phrase was chosen by Annie Ellsworth, the daughter of a friend, who had brought him the news that Congress had funded the line."},{"id":"inventors-3","section":"inventors","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Nobody would use a lift that might fall. So in 1854 Elisha Otis stood on a loaded platform hoisted above a crowd and had an assistant cut the rope with an axe. The safety brake caught. He sold lifts after that.","more":"Tall buildings only became worth building once people would agree to go up them."},{"id":"inventors-4","section":"inventors","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Cotton strips the soil. George Washington Carver spent his career persuading farmers to plant peanuts and sweet potatoes as well. Then he had to find uses for all those peanuts. So he published hundreds of recipes and products.","more":"He did not invent peanut butter, which existed before him. What he did was make a crop worth growing so the soil could recover."},{"id":"inventors-5","section":"inventors","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Garrett Morgan built a breathing hood for smoke. In 1916 he proved it worked. A tunnel under Lake Erie had collapsed and filled with gas, and he went down into it and brought men out. He later patented a traffic signal with a third position, one that stopped everybody at once.","more":"That third position is the reason traffic lights have a moment when all directions are red."},{"id":"inventors-6","section":"inventors","cat":"howworks","kind":"record","text":"Bell's telephone patent was filed in 1876. Another inventor, Elisha Gray, filed a description of something very similar on the same day. Which arrived first, and in what order they were logged, has been argued about ever since.","more":"Patents are records. This one is a good example of a record that is genuinely unclear, rather than simply unknown."},{"id":"everyday-1","section":"everyday","cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Before refrigeration, ice was a crop. Men cut it from frozen New England ponds in blocks and packed it in sawdust. It was shipped as far as India. Much of it arrived unmelted, and it made a fortune for the man who thought of it.","more":"His name was Frederic Tudor and everybody told him it was ridiculous. The first cargo mostly melted."},{"id":"everyday-2","section":"everyday","cat":"daily","kind":"found","text":"For about thirty years you could order a house from a catalogue. It arrived by rail as roughly 30,000 numbered pieces with a book of instructions, and you built it. Thousands are still standing and people live in them.","tryit":"Look up Sears kit houses and find the model called The Magnolia. Then work out what your own house would cost as a flat pack."},{"id":"everyday-3","section":"everyday","cat":"daily","kind":"found","text":"In 1935 about one farm in ten had electricity. Cities had been lit for fifty years. Running lines out to scattered farms cost more than any company could make back, so farm families formed their own cooperatives and strung the wire themselves.","more":"Many of those cooperatives still exist and still own the poles."},{"id":"everyday-4","section":"everyday","cat":"daily","kind":"found","text":"A one-room school held every age at once. The older children taught the younger ones, because the teacher could not be everywhere. Reading was drilled out of graded readers that everyone in the country used. A child who moved states carried on at the same page.","more":"Tens of thousands of the school buildings survive, mostly as houses now."},{"id":"everyday-5","section":"everyday","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Home canning is boiling food in a sealed jar until nothing living is left inside, then letting it cool so the lid is sucked down tight. The pop you hear when you open one is air going back in for the first time since it was sealed.","tryit":"Next time a jar is opened, listen for the pop. If there is no pop, the seal had already gone."},{"id":"everyday-6","section":"everyday","cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"The mail-order catalogue was, for a lot of families, the biggest book in the house apart from the Bible. Children learned to read from it, cut it up for paper dolls, and when a new one came the old one went out to the outhouse.","more":"Companies knew this. The catalogue was deliberately printed smaller than the almanac so it would sit on top of the pile and not get buried."},{"id":"machines-1","section":"machines","cat":"built","kind":"record","text":"The two railroads met at Promontory Summit in 1869. A telegraph wire was attached to the hammer and to the last spike. The blow itself sent a signal down the line. The word GONE went out to both coasts at the same moment.","more":"The man swinging the hammer missed on the first try. The telegraph operator sent the signal anyway."},{"id":"machines-2","section":"machines","cat":"when","kind":"record","text":"Before 1883 every town kept its own clock, set by the sun, so noon in one place was several minutes off noon in the next. Railway timetables became impossible. So the railroads simply invented four time zones and imposed them, and the country went along with it.","more":"The government did not make it law for another thirty-five years. The trains had already settled it."},{"id":"machines-3","section":"machines","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"A Mississippi steamboat drew so little water that captains joked they could run on heavy dew. Flat-bottomed hulls and paddle wheels let them work rivers a few feet deep, and they carried enormous loads.","more":"They also exploded regularly. Boiler pressure was pushed hard, and the wrecks of several are still in the mud of the riverbed, buried where the river later moved."},{"id":"machines-4","section":"machines","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Erie Canal ran 363 miles across New York State, dug largely by hand, with 83 locks to climb the height. Boats were pulled by mules walking a towpath beside the water. It cut the cost of moving a ton of freight by about ninety percent.","tryit":"Look at a map and find a route from the Hudson River to Lake Erie. Then imagine digging it with shovels and horses."},{"id":"machines-5","section":"machines","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Ford did not invent the car. He worked out how to stop the car moving around the factory and move the factory past the car instead. Building one chassis went from twelve and a half hours to ninety-three minutes.","more":"The idea came partly from meat-packing plants. There, carcasses already traveled on overhead rails, past men who each did one cut."},{"id":"machines-6","section":"machines","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The chief engineer of the Brooklyn Bridge was crippled by working in the pressurized chambers under the river. His wife Emily took over. She learned the mathematics herself. She carried his instructions to the site every day and dealt with the contractors, for eleven years.","more":"She was the first person to cross the finished bridge. Her name is on a plaque on it."},{"id":"flight-1","section":"flight","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The first flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 ft. That is less than the wingspan of a modern airliner. There were five witnesses and one photograph. The man who took it had never used a camera before, and was told simply to squeeze the bulb.","more":"That photograph came out perfectly. The aircraft itself hangs in the National Air and Space Museum in Washington."},{"id":"flight-2","section":"flight","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"The Wrights got there by not guessing. The published tables of wing performance kept giving them wrong results. So they built a wind tunnel out of a wooden box and a fan. They tested around two hundred wing shapes themselves and wrote down the numbers.","more":"The published tables really were wrong. Everyone else had been designing from them."},{"id":"flight-3","section":"flight","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Their real invention was not the wing or the engine. It was control. They twisted the whole wing to make the aircraft roll, having noticed how a buzzard tilts, and they worked the twist and the rudder together with one movement.","tryit":"Hold a long cardboard box by the ends and twist it gently. That is wing warping. Modern planes do the same job with hinged flaps at the wingtips."},{"id":"flight-4","section":"flight","cat":"daily","kind":"record","text":"The whole thing was paid for out of a bicycle shop. They built and sold bicycles in Dayton, Ohio, and spent the profits and the winters on airplanes. Bicycle work also gave them chains, sprockets, wire wheels and a feel for balance.","more":"They needed steady wind and soft sand to land on. So they shipped the aircraft to North Carolina in crates."},{"id":"flight-5","section":"flight","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The 1903 machine could barely fly. Two years later they had one that stayed up for 39 minutes and circled a field 30 times. That is the aircraft that actually mattered, and almost nobody can name it.","more":"It is called Flyer III, and it is on display in Dayton."},{"id":"flight-6","section":"flight","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"There is a bootprint on the moon that nobody will disturb. There is no wind and no rain, so it will stay as it was pressed. The astronauts also left mirrors, and laboratories on earth still bounce lasers off them to measure the distance to the moon.","more":"That measurement is how anyone knows the moon is moving away at about an inch and a half a year."},{"id":"frontier-1","section":"frontier","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"Near Guernsey, Wyoming, the Oregon Trail crossed a ridge of soft sandstone. So many iron-tyred wheels went over the same line that they cut a groove into the rock up to 5 ft deep. You can stand in it.","more":"Nobody dug it. It was worn, by wheels, one wagon at a time."},{"id":"frontier-2","section":"frontier","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Almost nobody rode in the wagons. They were full of supplies, and the ride was violent. The animals were usually oxen rather than horses. Oxen were cheaper and stronger, they ate worse grass, and they were less likely to be stolen. Families walked, for months.","tryit":"Work out how long 2,000 miles takes at 15 miles a day. That is the walk."},{"id":"frontier-3","section":"frontier","cat":"speed","kind":"record","text":"The Pony Express carried mail across nearly 2,000 miles in about ten days. Riders changed horses every 10 to 15 miles. It was famous immediately. It lasted eighteen months, because the telegraph reached the coast and made it pointless overnight.","more":"It never made money. It is remembered out of all proportion to how long it existed."},{"id":"frontier-4","section":"frontier","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"On the plains there was no timber, so houses were built out of the ground itself. Strips of turf were cut with a special plow and stacked like bricks, grass side down. The walls were about 2 ft thick, warm in winter, and they leaked mud when it rained.","more":"Photographs show families standing in front of them with a cow on the roof. That happened often enough to be a joke at the time."},{"id":"frontier-5","section":"frontier","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Two inventions made farming the dry plains possible. Barbed wire fenced land where there was no wood for rails. Windmills pumped water up from far below the ground with nobody doing the work.","more":"Both are still standing all over the American West. The windmills are the tall skeletal ones with a fantail that turns the wheel into the wind by itself."},{"id":"frontier-6","section":"frontier","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A chuckwagon was a kitchen on wheels. A hinged lid dropped down to make a work surface. Behind it were drawers for flour, coffee, beans and sourdough. The cook drove ahead of the herd, so food was ready when the riders arrived.","more":"Wooden axles kept breaking, so Charles Goodnight rebuilt an army wagon in iron. The design is credited to him."}];
globalThis.AMERICA = AMERICA;
